Sketch $y=|x^2-4x|$: the parabola $y=x^2-4x=(x-2)^2-4$ has roots $0$ and $4$ and minimum $-4$ at $x=2$. Reflecting the part below the axis gives a 'W' shape with zeros at $x=0,4$ and a local maximum of $4$ at $x=2$.

A horizontal line $y=k$ meets this graph:

* $k<0$: never; $k=0$: twice;
* $0<k<4$: four times (twice on the reflected arch, twice on the outer arms);
* $k=4$: three times (touching the top of the arch);
* $k>4$: twice.

So exactly four solutions when $0<k<4$.

*Algebra:* $x^2-4x=k$ always has two roots for $k>0$, and $x^2-4x=-k$ has two distinct roots iff $16-4k>0$, i.e. $k<4$.
